\begin{equation*}
\begin{split}
\mathbf{a} &= x_1 \mathbf{i} + x_2 \mathbf{j} + x_3 \mathbf{k}; \\
\mathbf{b} &= y_1 \mathbf{i} + y_2 \mathbf{j} + y_3 \mathbf{k}; \\
\|\mathbf{a}\| &= \sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2} = 2; \\
\|\mathbf{b}\| &= \sqrt{y_1^2 + y_2^2 + y_3^2} = 7; \\
\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} &= \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} = x_3(y_2 - y_3) \mathbf{i} + x_2(y_3 - x_1) \mathbf{j} + x_1(y_2 - x_2) \mathbf{k};
\end{split}
\end{equation*}
The cross product of the vectors is:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{split}
\|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}\| &= \sqrt{9 + 4 + 36} = \sqrt{49} = 7; \\
\|\mathbf{a}\| \|\mathbf{b}\| \cos{\theta} &= \|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}\| = 7; \\
2 \cdot 7 \cdot \cos{\theta} &= 7; \\
\cos{\theta} &= \frac{1}{2}; \\
\theta &= 60^{\circ};
\end{split}
\end{equation*}
The smallest possible angle between $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ is $\boxed{60^{\circ}}$.

